>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Students with locomotor disability in India have real career options, especially in desk-based and remote-friendly work such as software, marketing, design, accounting, tutoring and their own services, and the practical tests are physical access, exam facilities and proof of work.</strong> The decisions that unlock high income opportunities start with an honest access check and a high-income skill portfolio people can see.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Run the access check first.</strong> A 2022 government document shows access is uneven, so visit and ask before you accept a seat or an offer.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask early for facilities.</strong> CBSE recommends applying in Class IX and XI, and scribe and extra time rules apply to competitive exams.</li>
      <li><strong>Build proof.</strong> One finished project is the start of a skill portfolio and a faster path to financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide is part of our set of <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options for every stream and degree</a>. For other disabilities, read <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-blind-and-low-vision-students-india/">career options for blind and low-vision students</a> and <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-deaf-and-hard-of-hearing-students-india/">for deaf and hard of hearing students</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      <li><a href="#what">What counts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#access">Access check</a></li>
      <li><a href="#school">School exams</a></li>
      <li><a href="#exams">Entrance and recruitment exams</a></li>
      <li><a href="#quota">Quotas and professional courses</a></li>
      <li><a href="#routes">Eight routes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fieldwork">Roles with physical demands</a></li>
      <li><a href="#learn">Skills beside the credential</a></li>
      <li><a href="#money">Money</a></li>
      <li><a href="#checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</a></li>
      <li><a href="#gates">The 3 Gates</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mistakes">Expensive mistakes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next-step">What to do next</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQs</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="what">What counts as locomotor disability, and the papers that matter</h2>

  <p>
    Under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016, locomotor disability sits among the 21 recognised disabilities, alongside leprosy-cured persons, cerebral palsy, dwarfism, muscular dystrophy and acid-attack victims. Most school exam facilities and the government-job quota apply to benchmark disability, which means 40 percent or more of a specified disability certified by a competent authority.
  </p>
  <p>
    The UDID card is the document most schemes now ask for. The <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-students-with-disabilities-india/">main guide to career options for students with disabilities</a> explains how to apply for it.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Movement limits change which jobs are practical, and they do not decide how far you can go. The real constraints are usually physical access, travel and a handful of field-heavy roles, and all three can be checked before you commit. Work that happens on a laptop, a phone or a counter depends far less on mobility than people assume.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="access">The access check: do this before you pick a college or employer</h2>

  <p>
    A Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment document dated 3 December 2022 shows how uneven access still is. It reported that 8,33,703 of 11,68,292 government and aided schools (71 percent) were barrier-free, and that 1,45,747 buses (29.05 percent) were partially accessible while 8,695 (5.73 percent) were fully accessible.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same document says the Act sets five years for making existing buildings accessible and two years for services. So ask directly, and visit before you accept a seat or an offer.
  </p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Is there a ramp or lift to the classroom, library, lab and exam hall?</li>
    <li>Is there an accessible washroom on the same floor?</li>
    <li>Can the hostel or a nearby rental support a wheelchair or crutches?</li>
    <li>Is the daily commute workable, and what is the cost?</li>
    <li>Can classes or work be arranged on the ground floor or online when needed?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note-line">
    Figures are from the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment document of 3 December 2022 and may have changed. Ask each institution for its current access audit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="school">School board exams: what CBSE allows</h2>

  <p>
    CBSE's circular of 24 January 2017 covers candidates with disabilities under the 2016 Act, including those it calls physically handicapped. Ask your school for the version in force this year.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Facility</th>
          <th>What the circular says</th>
          <th>What to do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Facility"><strong>Scribe or extra time</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the circular says">A scribe, compensatory time or both. For a three-hour paper the extra time is 60 minutes, and it is 50 minutes for 2.5 hours, 40 for two hours and 30 for 1.5 hours. The scribe should not hold qualifications in the subject, and the candidate may choose the scribe.</td>
          <td data-label="What to do">Ask the school to record the facility at registration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Facility"><strong>Fewer languages</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the circular says">Candidates with 2016 Act disabilities may study one compulsory language instead of two, within the three-language formula, and then choose four other subjects.</td>
          <td data-label="What to do">Check how this affects college eligibility before you choose.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Facility"><strong>Physical education</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the circular says">Physiotherapy exercises are treated as equivalent to the Board's Physical and Health Education course.</td>
          <td data-label="What to do">Ask the school to record your physiotherapy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Facility"><strong>Third language (Classes VI to VIII)</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the circular says">Exemption is available to candidates with disabilities defined in the 2016 Act.</td>
          <td data-label="What to do">Apply through the school.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Facility"><strong>How to apply</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the circular says">Apply through the school head to the Regional Office with a medical certificate, preferably in Class IX or XI. Only students recorded in those classes are considered.</td>
          <td data-label="What to do">Do not wait for Class X or XII.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">
    Source: CBSE circular dated 24 January 2017, read on 30 October 2026. The circular makes no separate provision for ramps or exam-centre allotment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exams">Entrance and recruitment exams: scribes and extra time</h2>

  <p>
    A Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities memorandum of 10 August 2022 covers candidates who have difficulty in writing. It says compensatory time should be not less than 20 minutes per hour, and that the candidate may choose their own scribe or ask the exam body for one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Supreme Court held on 3 February 2025 in Gulshan Kumar v. IBPS that denying a scribe or compensatory time constitutes discrimination under the 2016 Act. Apply for the facility when you apply for the exam, and attach the certificate in the format the notice asks for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quota">Seats, posts and professional courses</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Area</th>
          <th>What the sources say</th>
          <th>What to check</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Area"><strong>Higher education seats</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the sources say">The Act reserves 5 percent of seats in government and government-aided higher-education institutions for students with benchmark disabilities.</td>
          <td data-label="What to check">Check the admission notice for how the quota is split and what certificate is needed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Area"><strong>Government jobs</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the sources say">A DoPT order of 15 January 2018 reserves 4 percent of direct-recruitment vacancies, with 1 percent for locomotor disability including cerebral palsy, leprosy cured, dwarfism, acid-attack victims and muscular dystrophy. It applies only to posts identified as suitable.</td>
          <td data-label="What to check">Read the identified-post list in each notice.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Area"><strong>MBBS and medical courses</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What the sources say">In Omkar Ramchandra Gond v. Union of India (15 October 2024) the Supreme Court held that the mere existence of a benchmark disability will not disqualify a candidate, and that assessment boards must record whether the disability prevents the course. The NMC's 2026 guidelines describe a competency-based assessment with separate affidavit formats for upper-limb and lower-limb locomotor disability.</td>
          <td data-label="What to check">Read the current NMC guidelines and the counselling instructions.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="routes">Eight routes, strongest growth first</h2>

  <p>
    The routes are ordered by how far visible skill can lift income, with rank-based routes last. Many of the strongest ones can be done on a laptop, from home or a nearby co-working space, which reduces the travel and access problem.
  </p>
  <div class="route-grid">
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">01</span>
        <span class="chip chip--high">Scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Software, data and automation</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Clients and employers pay for working products, and much of the work can be done remotely or in a flexible set-up.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Software firms, startups and clients who buy finished projects.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>A live project, a clear explanation and a referral from someone who used it.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Entry-level coding is the first work AI tools absorb. Aim for judgement work on a real business problem.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Learn one language or a spreadsheet-and-SQL stack, build three projects for one industry and take small paid work. First sales skill: one line saying who your work helps.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">02</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Digital marketing, e-commerce and online sales</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Results are numbers that employers and clients can check, and the work can be done from home.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Shops, brands and online sellers; later, your own store or clients.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Lead and order numbers, before-and-after cases and referrals.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Commission-only offers that ask you to pay first are a common trap.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Choose a product type, learn the numbers and take on accounts. First sales skill: follow up after every no.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">03</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Design, video and content</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">A portfolio opens doors that a certificate does not, and project work can be done on your schedule.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Studios, brands and direct clients.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Three finished pieces for one kind of client.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>AI drafts basic visuals and edits, so your edge is taste and reliability.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Pick a niche and offer a fixed-price package.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">04</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Accounting, finance and compliance</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Desk-based professional work with clear qualifications, and it can grow into an independent practice.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Firms, small businesses and individuals.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Qualifications, accurate work and referrals.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Professional courses have long exam stages and their own exam-facility rules.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Start with bookkeeping and tax work for small clients and build toward a practice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">05</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Teaching, tutoring and training online</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Online tutoring removes the commute and lets you start with a few students.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Students, parents, schools and training firms.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Student results and recommendations.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Teaching posts have their own qualification rules.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Start with one subject and ask each student for a referral.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">06</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Own business and local services</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">A home-based or near-home service, such as tuition, repair, tailoring, food or resale, can start small.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Local households, shops and online customers.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Repeat orders and word of mouth.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Do not borrow heavily before customers have paid you.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Start with one service, keep a customer list and ask for referrals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">07</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Customer support, operations and HR</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Many of these roles are desk-based, and employers hire on communication and reliability.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>IT/ITES firms, banks and service companies.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Supervisor references and quality scores.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Pay starts modest. Ask about the first promotion step and the office access.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Move toward team lead, training or operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">08</span>
        <span class="chip chip--low">Not so scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Government and public-sector exams</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Selection is by ranked exams, and 1 percent of reserved vacancies are for locomotor disability in identified posts.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Central and state departments, as each notice states.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Your exam rank and documents.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Two hard stages: a competitive written exam and a limited number of identified posts. Keep a parallel skill.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Read the identified-post list, set a review date with your family and keep a skill that earns outside the exam.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> a small online or home-based service, such as bookkeeping for local shops, tutoring, design or an e-commerce store.
    </p>
    <p>
      Write the price and what is included for every job. Keep a customer list and add a helper when orders exceed your hours.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fieldwork">Roles with physical demands: how to test them honestly</h2>

  <p>
    Some roles involve long standing, lifting, site visits or heavy equipment, such as certain engineering, healthcare and field jobs. Do not rule these in or out on assumption.
  </p>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Ask someone doing the job what a normal week looks like.</li>
    <li>Try a short placement or shadowing day with your own aids.</li>
    <li>Ask what adjustments the employer or college has made for others.</li>
    <li>Look for a nearby role that uses the same knowledge with different physical demands.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For regulated courses, the assessment rules above ask for a function-based decision made with reasonable accommodation, and they require written reasons for any refusal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="learn">Building skills from home or on campus: free routes first</h2>

  <p>
    Online learning removes travel, and for many wheelchair users and people with mobility limits it opens fields faster than a campus can. The question is whether the platform and the work suit your body and your budget.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>YouTube lessons for beginner coding, design, accounts, writing and spreadsheets.</li>
    <li>Free public courses on SWAYAM and NPTEL, with a paid exam only if you need the certificate.</li>
    <li>Official documentation and communities that review work online.</li>
    <li>Remote internships and paid trial tasks from employers who publish clear job notices.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Test one lesson before you pay. Check the teacher, the last update, the practice and feedback, the refund terms, and whether live sessions have recordings if fatigue or health days interrupt you.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    Plan hours around your energy, not a fixed number. Many students start with a short daily block and add time as the habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, finish one small output that shows the skill, and show it to a practitioner. Ask for one correction and apply it.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A physical limit does not cap skill or income, and a high-income skill portfolio can be built remotely. Pair a subject skill with clear communication, a sense of who pays and a budget the family can hold, and an earlier financial freedom stays possible.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Money, devices and fee traps</h2>

  <p>
    Assistive devices, a better wheelchair or prosthesis and accessible transport are part of your career toolkit. Look for central and state schemes and scholarships for students with disabilities, and check each scheme's conditions before you plan around it.
  </p>
  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution. Be careful with centres that promise guaranteed placement for a fee.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 10 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 1 lakh for the degree fee. The rest can fund a device or mobility aid, a laptop, one skill course, accessible transport for placements and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p class="note-line">
    We give no salary figures because we found no dependable source. Read live job ads and ask people in the work.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for choosing a field</h2>

  <p>
    Test each route with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: what suits your body and energy?</div>
        <p>Notice how long you can work comfortably and what recovery you need. Match the route to your real daily capacity, not to an ideal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: access, time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Check commute, building access, device costs and who can help with travel or paperwork.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: do employers hire this way?</div>
        <p>Read ten notices for your target role and ask about remote or flexible set-ups.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Routine tasks shrink first. Pick work that needs judgement, domain knowledge and customer contact.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gates">The 3 Gates: proof before commitment</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The 3 Gates</strong> check that your effort turns into work other people rely on.
  </p>
  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>Complete one paid or practice job from your own set-up, at your own pace, and keep the result.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof you can explain it</span>
        <p>Write a short note on what you delivered, in how many hours and on what tools.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof someone will pay</span>
        <p>Ask a first customer to pay a fair price, or to give a written testimonial after delivery.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Choosing a college without visiting</div>
        <p>A stated "accessible campus" can still mean steps to the lab or no accessible washroom.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Waiting until Class X or XII to apply for facilities</div>
        <p>CBSE recommends applying in Class IX and XI.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Ignoring the identified-post list</div>
        <p>Reserved posts apply only where the post is identified as suitable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Paying for a placement promise</div>
        <p>No centre can guarantee a job. Ask for verified outcomes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">05</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Letting others rule out a field</div>
        <p>Test it yourself with a short trial and your own aids.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Get your UDID and certificate in order, run the access check on your top two options and ask for the exam facilities you will need. Start one small project beside your studies.
  </p>
  <p>
    A specialist can look at your interests, access needs and family situation with you. A sensible first step is <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/student-career-guidance/">student career guidance</a>. The aim is skills that pay well and an earlier path to financial freedom.
  </p>

